      "result": {
        "reply_text": "I understand that you are working at a private shop in Kanpur and have not received your salary of 14,000 rupees for October 2026. You are still employed at the same shop.\n\nEvery employer is responsible for paying the wages due to their employees. If an employer fails to pay wages, the proprietor of the establishment is responsible for the payment.\n\nFor employees paid on a monthly basis, the employer must pay wages before the expiry of the seventh day of the following month.\n\nIf your wages are not paid, you may file a claim with the authority appointed by the government to hear such cases. This authority has the power to order the payment of your claim and may also order additional compensation. If the employer fails to pay the amount ordered, the authority can issue a recovery certificate to the District Magistrate to recover the money as arrears of land revenue.\n\nYou should keep a record of your employment, such as your appointment letter, salary slips, or any communication with your employer regarding the unpaid salary. It is also helpful to keep a copy of your attendance records if you have them.\n\nYou may first try to speak with your employer to resolve the issue. If that does not work, you can approach the local labour office in Kanpur to inquire about the specific authority appointed to handle wage claims in your area.\n\nHave you raised this issue with your employer yet? Do you have any proof of employment like an appointment letter or salary slip?\n\nSource: Code on Wages, 2019, section 43\nSource: Code on Wages, 2019, section 17\nSource: Code on Wages, 2019, section 45\n\nNote: Payment of Wages Act, 1936 is no longer in force, so I do not use it.\n\nWhat I could not verify:\n- The specific contact details or office address for the labour authority in Kanpur.\n- The exact procedure for filing a claim in your specific district.\n\nThis is legal information, not legal advice.",
